From 4abcb017f2c77cde470050c49d12effcd378845a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Roger Sayle <roger@nextmovesoftware.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 14:30:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] PR middle-end/126952: --enable-checking=rtl bootstrap
 regression

This is the minimal fix to restore --enable-checking=rtl builds.
Conceptually, wide-int has a simple invariant: if len is greater than
zero, then the val field is well-defined, but if len is zero, val may
be uninitialized.  Alas, wi::copy doesn't correctly defend against
the case that len is zero, resulting in a "maybe used uninitialized"
error during bootstrap.  Reducing a testcase is difficult, but the
closest I can reduce looks something like:

void foo(unsigned int len, int *ptr)
{
  int *undef;
  int *val = len ? ptr : undef;
  int i = 0;
  do {
    val[i]++;
  } while (++i < len);
}

Notice that when len is zero, val's value is undefined and that this
undefined pointer is unconditionally dereferenced.  This regression
was exposed on mainline, by a change to thread backedges which peels
the first iteration of the loop, revealing the path where val is
always (obviously) undefined.  In reality, this analysis depends upon
various inlining decisions, silencing the "maybe used uninitialized"
when the logic is sufficiently complex.

2026-11-10  Roger Sayle  <roger@nextmovesoftware.com>

gcc/ChangeLog
	PR middle-end/126952
	* wide-int.h (wi::copy): Check that len is non-zero before
	copying the contents of wide-int.

Signed-off-by: Zhongteng Gui <dragon-archer@outlook.com>
---
 gcc/wide-int.h | 15 +++++++++------
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gcc/wide-int.h b/gcc/wide-int.h
index 59b2796ed95..41bf3418b20 100644
--- a/gcc/wide-int.h
+++ b/gcc/wide-int.h
@@ -2182,12 +2182,15 @@ inline void
 wi::copy (T1 &x, const T2 &y)
 {
   unsigned int len = y.get_len ();
-  HOST_WIDE_INT *xval = x.write_val (len);
-  const HOST_WIDE_INT *yval = y.get_val ();
-  unsigned int i = 0;
-  do
-    xval[i] = yval[i];
-  while (++i < len);
+  if (len > 0)
+    {
+      HOST_WIDE_INT *xval = x.write_val (len);
+      const HOST_WIDE_INT *yval = y.get_val ();
+      unsigned int i = 0;
+      do
+        xval[i] = yval[i];
+      while (++i < len);
+    }
   /* For widest_int write_val is called with an exact value, not
      upper bound for len, so nothing is needed further.  */
   if (!wi::int_traits <T1>::needs_write_val_arg)
-- 
2.56.0.windows.4

